<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aksumite Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aksum, Kingdom of Aksum, Aksumites, Aksumite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aksumite Empire was a trading state centred on Aksum in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, flourishing from roughly the first to the seventh century CE. Through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adulis">Adulis</a> it traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, minted its own coins and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">King Ezana</a>. It is regarded as an ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and contributed to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire.jpg" alt="Aksumite Empire" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KingEndybisEthiopia227-235CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
